{
    "_what": "Every entry point a person or an agent can use, assigned. A VS Code command, a webview message type (every handler map, every direct registration, the sidebar) and an MCP tool are read out of src/ by tests/templates/entryPointScan.ts; tests/templates/action-doors-coverage.test.ts fails on any not assigned here, and on any assigned here that no longer exists. A descriptor tool ({ tool, type } row) is the same entry point as its handler type, so it is covered by that type and not listed. Companion of action-doors.ledger.json, whose action ids `actions` names.",
    "_rules": [
        "Each entry point is in exactly one place: a bucket's `members`, `forwards`, or `actions`.",
        "`actions` maps an entry point to the action rows (action-doors.ledger.json) whose spine it reaches. Its file is then one of that row's doors, checked both ways there.",
        "`forwards` maps an entry point whose whole job is to run another listed entry point to that target. The target must be enumerated, and a command target's id must appear in a file that registers the forwarder.",
        "A `read` entry point whose own declaration says it writes (`readOnly: false`, `readOnlyHint: false`) fails, and so does a declared-read-only tool or descriptor type placed in `write` or `actions`.",
        "`drift` marks an assigned entry point that disagrees with another door of the same job; it names its fix, and counts toward action-doors.ledger.json `exemptionCeiling`.",
        "`sharedNames`: a message type registered by several maps with DIFFERENT implementations is two doors under one name. Each needs a reason; one that becomes a single implementation is stale.",
        "A new entry point: add it here in the change that registers it. A write with a second entry point doing the same job gets an action row instead of `write`."
    ],
